ry closely at a pan of water just below boiling, you might sometimes see faint wisps right at the surface where some vapor condenses briefly, but it vanishes almost instantly. So the stove experiment actually shows: - Before boiling: slow, invisible evaporation (the water level slowly goes down even before it boils). - At boiling: massive, violent production of invisible vapor that condenses into a visible cloud of droplets. Your memory of school wasn't wrong—it was just incomplete. They taught you the *boiling point*, but not that evaporation is a separate, invisible process happening all along. Thanks for pushing back on this—it's a perfect example of how our senses can trick us, and clarifying it makes the science even cooler.
